const TEACHING = "Read the Game (Pack 1, level L3, version 1.1.0)\nPromise: See danger two passes early and be in the right place before the shot exists.\n\n## The Keeper's Mind — Section 01\nTechnique gets you into the team. Understanding keeps you there. The step you are taking now is from a keeper who stops shots to a keeper who changes what happens in front of him.\n\nUp to now most of your development has been about the question \"Can I do it?\" — can I catch it, can I dive, can I kick it far. You can. The next question is harder and much more interesting: \"Why am I doing it, and what should happen next?\"\nAt your level, the keepers who stand out are not always the ones with the best hands. They are the ones who are never surprised. They saw the cross before the winger did. They were already set when the shot came. They told the defender to step before the striker got the ball. That is not luck and it is not talent — it is reading the game, and it can be trained like any other skill.\n\nThe loop that runs every action\n1. See — What is actually happening? Ball, bodies, space, who is free.\n2. Understand — What does that mean? Where is the danger going to come from in the next two seconds?\n3. Decide & Execute — Pick one action and commit to it fully. A half-decision is the only truly wrong one.\n4. Review & Adapt — Did it work? Why or why not? Adjust before the next one — not after the match.\n\nThis loop runs dozens of times every match, mostly when the ball is nowhere near you. The quality of your See and Understand decides the quality of everything else. A keeper who sees late has to be a superhero to stop the shot. A keeper who sees early just has to be in the right place.\n\nFour things a thinking keeper does differently\n- Positions before the ball moves — tag: Before · He adjusts on every pass, not on every shot. By the time the shot comes, the positioning is already done and all that is left is the save.\n- Reads intentions, not just the ball — tag: During · Where is the striker's head looking? Is his hip open to shoot or to pass? Is the winger's touch long or short? These cues arrive before the ball does.\n- Controls the players in front of him — tag: Around · He is the only player who sees the whole picture. Used well, that view is worth more than any save. See Section 06.\n- Owns every outcome — tag: After · Good or bad, he knows why it happened. No excuses, no hiding, no drama. 'I got that wrong, this is why, this is what I'll do next time.'\n\nConfidence is a decision, not a feeling: Confidence does not arrive after a good save; it is what makes the good save possible. On the pitch it looks like this: chest open, chin up, voice early, moving to every ball with intent — even when you are nervous, even after a mistake. The body leads and the feeling follows. Your defenders read your body language constantly. If you look sure, they play sure.\n\nMistakes\nYou will make them. Every keeper at every level does, and the position is designed so that yours are the visible ones. What separates keepers is the next ten seconds. The best keepers have a short, almost boring routine: one breath, one look at the next job (where is the ball, what is the restart), one instruction to the team. Then the mistake is over. It gets reviewed later, honestly, with a coach — not during the match in your own head.\nA conceded goal is also information. Where did it come from? What did you see late? What could a call from you have stopped? That is a far better use of the walk back to your line than replaying the shot.\n\n\"I am not just protecting the goal. I am influencing the team.\" — The question that changes at this stage of a keeper's development\n## Reading the Game — Section 02\nDanger has a shape, and it shows itself early. Your job is to spot the shape while there is still time to do something about it.\n\nMost goals at your level are not unstoppable shots. They are a keeper or a defence that saw something half a second late: a runner nobody tracked, a cross nobody attacked, a keeper who set his feet after the shot was struck. Reading the game is the skill of seeing those moments before they happen.\nIt starts with one habit: scanning. Every time the ball travels, your eyes should do a quick loop — ball, nearest attacker, far-side runner, your own line — and come back to the ball. Two or three scans a minute when the ball is far away; constant when it is in your half.\n\nThe cues that arrive before the ball does\n- Hips and shoulders — tag: Body · A player can only pass or shoot where his hips point. Open hips facing goal = shot or cross coming. Closed hips = he's turning back or playing sideways. Watch the hips, not the feet.\n- The head — tag: Body · A player looking up has a picture and is about to use it — expect a pass or a cross. A player looking down at the ball has no picture — pressure him, or expect a dribble.\n- The touch — tag: Ball · A heavy first touch means the player needs an extra step: time to close the angle or for a defender to step in. A clean touch into stride means he's ready to act now — be set.\n- Pass speed and height — tag: Ball · A slow, bobbling pass into the box is a chance to come and take it. A driven low pass across the box is a cut-back — expect a first-time shot from the second runner.\n- Where the space is — tag: Space · Before the ball moves, ask: if it goes to that space, who gets there first? If the answer is 'their player', the call to your defender must happen now.\n- The far side — tag: Space · The most dangerous player is usually the one furthest from the ball. Wingers ghosting in at the back post score more than strikers in the middle. Know where he is.\n\nThe four phases of play — and what you do in each\n- We have the ball (build-up) — where: High and wide of your goal — often at or outside the edge of the box, offering a back-pass option. · see: Which of our defenders is free? Where is their striker pressing from? Is there a safe pass if the ball comes back to me? · do: Keep moving to give a clean angle for the back pass. Already know my next pass before I receive. Be the calm option, not the panic one. · say: \"Time\" if the defender has it. \"Man on\" if he doesn't. \"Keeper's here\" to offer the pass back. Point where I want the ball played.\n- We lose the ball (transition to defend) — where: Immediately drop and narrow — the first three seconds after losing the ball are the most dangerous in football. · see: Who is the free runner? Is there a direct ball coming over the top? Is my line still high and now exposed? · do: Sprint, don't jog, to my starting position. If the ball is going long and I can get there first — go. Otherwise get set early. · say: \"Drop!\" or \"Recover!\" loud and once. Name the runner: \"Thabo — runner, left!\"\n- They have the ball (organised defending) — where: Starting position based on where the ball is: deeper and more central when it's far, higher and on the angle when it's close. · see: The shape of my back line. Gaps between defenders. Who's marking who. Where the cross or shot will come from. · do: Adjust on every pass. Stay on my toes. Be set at the moment the ball could be struck — not before, not after. · say: Run the shape: \"Step up\", \"Hold\", \"Squeeze\", \"Tuck in\". Mark the danger: \"Back post!\", \"Second ball!\"\n- We win the ball (transition to attack) — where: If I have it: ready to release fast. If a defender has it: moving to offer the outlet. · see: Is the other team disorganised? Is there a fast forward in space? Or are they already set — meaning we should keep the ball and build? · do: Decide in one touch: quick (throw or kick to the free player while they're unbalanced) or slow (keep possession, let the team move up). · say: \"Go, go!\" if we have a counter. \"Easy — keep it\" if we don't. Name the target: \"Lerato, wide!\"\n\nTriggers: the moments that demand a decision\nA trigger is a moment where the picture changes and you must act in the next second. Learn to recognise these and your reactions become anticipations.\n- Ball played over or behind your defence → sweep or set? (Section 04)\n- Attacker's head goes up on the wing → cross is coming: position, call, and claim or hold.\n- Defender takes a touch under pressure facing his own goal → back pass is coming: show for it and know where it's going next.\n- Attacker gets side-on with the ball at his feet inside the box → shot or cut-back: set, and tell the defender to block.\n- Our full-back goes forward and the ball is lost → the space he left is the danger: who fills it? Call it.\n\nWords you'll hear — and should use\n- Starting position — Where you stand before the next action, based on where the ball is. · when: All the time the ball is in play.\n- Set — Feet shoulder-width, weight on the balls of the feet, hands ready, still — at the moment of the strike. · when: Any time a shot could come.\n- Angle — Your position on the line between the ball and the middle of the goal. · when: Shot-stopping and 1v1s.\n- Depth — How far off your line you are. · when: Balancing shot-stopping against crosses and through balls.\n- Scan — A quick look away from the ball to check positions. · when: Every time the ball travels.\n- Second ball — Where the ball goes after a header, block, or save. · when: Crosses, set pieces, long balls.\n- Cut-back — A pass pulled back from the by-line to a runner near the penalty spot. · when: Attacker reaches the by-line.\n- Transition — The few seconds after possession changes. · when: Constantly — and it's where most goals start.\n- Sweeping — Leaving your box to deal with a ball played behind the defence. · when: High line, ball over the top.\n- Line — Your defenders, as a unit. 'The line' is both where they stand and the shape they hold. · when: Organising the defence.\n## Positioning & Angles — Section 03\nA keeper in the right place makes hard saves look easy and never needs to make the spectacular ones. Positioning is the quietest skill and the most important.\n\nThink of positioning as two separate questions you answer on every pass:\n- Angle — am I on the line between the ball and the centre of the goal?\n- Depth — how far off my line should I be?\nAngle is nearly always the same answer: find the imaginary line from the ball to the middle of the goal and stand on it. Depth is the real decision, and it changes with where the ball is, who has it, and how far away your defenders are.\n\nThe three positioning errors every young keeper makes\n- Standing on the line — The line is a place to finish, not to start. Standing on it gives the shooter the full goal and takes away your ability to sweep. Start off it; only go to it for corners and when a shot is certain.\n- Setting too late — If your feet are still moving when the ball is struck, you will dive from a bad base and go late. Set is a moment, not a place: it happens the instant the ball could be hit.\n- Following the ball, not the angle — When the ball moves across the box, moving sideways on the line leaves you square to a ball that is now hitting from an angle. Move in an arc — forward as the ball comes central, back as it goes wide.\n\nDepth: the trade-off you're always managing\nFurther off your line = better angle against shots and better chance to sweep, but more exposed to chips and lobs and later to crosses. Closer to your line = safer against the lob and the cross, but the shooter sees more goal and the through-ball becomes your defender's problem alone.\nAt this level the common mistake is being too deep, not too high. Most strikers can't reliably chip or lob from distance, and most dangerous moments are balls behind the defence. Err towards being higher, especially when the ball is far away — and work back towards your goal as the ball comes closer.\n\nThe arc: Picture a curved line from one post, out to about the penalty spot, and back to the other post. As the ball moves across the pitch, you move along that arc, always on the ball-to-centre-of-goal line. Central ball = top of the arc, furthest out. Wide ball = down near the post. Moving along the arc keeps your angle right without you having to think about it.\n## On the Pitch — On the pitch\nReading is trained by looking. These tasks point your eyes at the right things in real sessions and matches — nothing here can be done on a sofa.\n\nReading is trained by looking. These tasks point your eyes at the right things in real sessions and matches — nothing here can be done on a sofa.\nCheck in after each session or match — a date, did it happen (yes, partly, no — all honest answers), and what you noticed. The record is yours. Nobody is checking; the point is that you are.\n\nWhy this pack can't be finished indoors: Reading, deciding and organising are habits, and habits are built on grass. The lessons and the simulator show you what good looks like; only matches and sessions make it yours. Completing a pack means it has shown up in your game — that is the whole point.";
const CLIENT = "Visuals on the site (client-only; decisions made there arrive as events in the shared document, not through a tool):\n- checklist \"Habits to build this season\" (5 items) → #s/read-the-game/mind/mind-habits\n- pitch diagram \"Starting positions by ball location\" → #s/read-the-game/positioning/positioning-pitch\n- quiz \"Positioning check\" (4 questions) → #s/read-the-game/positioning/positioning-check";
const FIELD = "Field tasks (#s/read-the-game/on-the-pitch/field-read-the-game). Check-ins are honest self-reports; no proof, no policing.\n- scan-count (do, session): Every time the ball travels in a small-sided game, do one scan: ball → nearest attacker → far-side runner → my line → ball.\n  why: Scanning is the habit everything else depends on. It feels pointless when the ball is far away — that is exactly when it matters.\n  ask: Did it become automatic or did you have to remind yourself? When did you forget?\n- hips-head (notice, match): For the whole first half, watch the hips and head of whoever has the ball in your half. Call what's coming out loud before it happens: \"shot\", \"cross\", \"pass\".\n  why: Intentions arrive before the ball does. Calling them out loud forces you to read early and tells your defenders at the same time.\n  ask: How often were you right? Which cue fooled you?\n- far-side (notice, match): Each time the ball goes wide, find the furthest-away attacker before you look back at the ball.\n  why: The back-post runner is the most common unseen goal at youth level. He is only invisible if you never look.\n  ask: Was anyone ever free at the back post? Did you say anything?\n- starting-position (do, session): In a game, move to a new starting position on EVERY pass the other team makes, even in their half. Never be standing still when the ball moves.\n  why: Positioning is done before the shot, not during it. A keeper who adjusts on every pass never has to make the spectacular save.\n  ask: Did your coach or teammates notice? Where did you catch yourself standing on the line?\n- walk-the-pitch (notice, match): Before kick-off, walk your box: bounce, grass, sun, wind. Decide one thing you'll do differently because of it.\n  why: Conditions change decisions — a wet pitch means parry wide, a low sun means come for fewer crosses. Pros do this every match.\n  ask: What did you notice, and what did you change?\n\nDrills (#s/read-the-game/on-the-pitch/drills-read-the-game):\n- arc-shadow \"Shadow the arc\" (solo, 8 min)\n  setup: A goal (or two cones 7m apart) and three markers on an arc: one central ~11m out, two wide ~6m out level with the posts.\n  steps: Start on your line, centre. / Imagine the ball at the central marker — move to your set position on the ball-to-centre line, get set, hold 2 seconds. / Imagine it switched wide left — move along the arc, set, hold. Then wide right. Then back central. / Three rounds slow, three rounds at match pace. Set means still: feet shoulder-width, weight forward, hands ready.\n  focus: Arrive and be still before the \"shot\". If your feet are moving at the hold, you were late.\n  game link: This is the movement you make a hundred times a match while nothing seems to be happening. It is why good keepers look like they are never diving far.\n- call-the-cue \"Call the cue\" (pair, 10 min)\n  setup: A partner with a ball 15–20m from goal, you in goal.\n  steps: Partner dribbles slowly and, without warning, either shoots, passes to the side, or turns away. / You call it — \"shot!\", \"pass!\", \"turn!\" — the moment you read it from hips and head, before they act. / Partner tells you if the call came before or after the action. Ten reps, swap. / Progress: partner adds a fake. Only react to the hips, never the eyes or the arms.\n  focus: Hips first. A player can only strike where his hips point.\n  game link: Every 1v1 and every edge-of-box shot is decided by whether you read the strike early enough to be set.\n- scan-game \"Scan-and-call game\" (group, 12 min)\n  setup: Any small-sided game at training. You in goal. One extra rule for you only.\n  steps: Every time the ball crosses the halfway line towards you, you must say out loud where the furthest attacker is: \"far side left\", \"back post\". / If a coach or teammate catches you silent, that's a point against you. Count them. / Second game: add the shape — \"step up\" or \"drop\" — every time possession changes.\n  focus: Saying it proves you saw it. Silence means you were ball-watching.\n  game link: This is the exact habit that turns into organising the defence in Pack 3.";

export const checkinTool = defineTool({
	name: "checkin_read-the-game",
	description: "Record a field check-in for the Read the Game pack after a session or match. One check-in per task per date. Tasks: scan-count, hips-head, far-side, starting-position, walk-the-pitch.",
	parameters: Type.Object({
		task: Type.Union([Type.Literal("scan-count"), Type.Literal("hips-head"), Type.Literal("far-side"), Type.Literal("starting-position"), Type.Literal("walk-the-pitch")]),
		date: Type.String({ description: "ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD) of the session or match" }),
		done: Type.Union([Type.Literal("yes"), Type.Literal("partly"), Type.Literal("no")]),
		notes: Type.Optional(Type.String()),
	}),
	execute: async (args, api, context) => {
		const keeperId = keeperIdOf({ conversationId: api.conversationId, agent: await api.agent(context) });
		const event = await api.commit((tx) => appendEvent(tx, keeperId, "field.checkin", { task: args.task, date: args.date, done: args.done, notes: args.notes ?? "" }, CTX), context);
		return { content: [{ type: "text", text: `recorded field.checkin ${args.task} ${args.date} ${args.done} (t=${event.t})` }] };
	},
});

export const logDrillTool = defineTool({
	name: "log_drill_read-the-game",
	description: "Log a drill session for the Read the Game pack. Drills: arc-shadow, call-the-cue, scan-game. Rating 1-5 is the keeper's own feel.",
	parameters: Type.Object({
		drill: Type.Union([Type.Literal("arc-shadow"), Type.Literal("call-the-cue"), Type.Literal("scan-game")]),
		date: Type.String({ description: "ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD)" }),
		rating: Type.Integer({ minimum: 1, maximum: 5 }),
		notes: Type.Optional(Type.String()),
	}),
	execute: async (args, api, context) => {
		const keeperId = keeperIdOf({ conversationId: api.conversationId, agent: await api.agent(context) });
		const event = await api.commit((tx) => appendEvent(tx, keeperId, "drill.logged", { drill: args.drill, date: args.date, rating: args.rating, notes: args.notes ?? "" }, CTX), context);
		return { content: [{ type: "text", text: `recorded drill.logged ${args.drill} ${args.date} rating ${args.rating} (t=${event.t})` }] };
	},
});

